    "q-23288": {
      "dom_id": "step-q23288",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "What are the coordinates of the minimum point of the function $y={x}^{2}+3?$",
      "worked_solution": "In general, the graph of $y={x}^{2}+k$ looks like the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ but translated $k$ units up. The vertex of $y={x}^{2}+k$ is located at $(0,k).$\n\nFor the given function $y={x}^{2}+3,$ we have $k=3.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ after being translated $3$ units *up*. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218419/assets/b66bc0862b1a16cd71e846624903adea3f98a1b6d867a6516ec08cdee970518c.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(0,3),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point.",
