    "q-105994": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105994",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "What are the coordinates of the minimum point of the function $y=(x-2)^{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "In general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^{2}$ looks like the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ but translated $h$ units right. The vertex of $y=(x-h)^{2}$ is located at $(h,0).$\n\nFor the given function $y=(x-2)^{2},$ we have $h=2.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ after being translated $2$ units *right*. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218419/assets/aed4cce9a698952f9d24e386249c88023d4e56a353f2d7e0c6b75f3bec0cdf4d.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(2,0),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-105994-a",
              "type": "math",
              "value": "(2,0)",
              "html": "<mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"35\" style=\"font-size: 119.6%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c28\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c29\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container>"
            },
            {
              "option": "b",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-105994-b",
